{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "423",
    "case_summary": "A 73-year-old man presented with intractable hiccups. His past medical history included fatigue several years prior, when he received chemotherapy (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, and prednisone) followed by autologous peripheral blood stem cell transplant. Serial monitoring thereafter revealed normal complete blood cell counts.\n\nThe patient was afebrile with normal vital signs. Neurologic examination findings were normal except for the presence of hiccups.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    Brain MRI with gadolinium showed:    - Diffuse leptomeningeal enhancement    - Enhancement of several cranial nerves including bilateral vagus nerves    Image Title: Figure 1. Leptomeningeal Enhancement Associated With Intractable Hiccups    Image Description: A) Nodular enhancement involving cisternal segments of bilateral glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves, right hypoglossal nerve, intraparenchymal ventral medullary enhancement, and extensive leptomeningeal enhancement. B) Follow-up showing improvement after treatment.  - Laboratory Tests:    Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showed:    - Lymphocytosis with plasmacytoid features on light microscopy    - Flow cytometry showed κ light chain–restricted B-cell lymphoma with plasmacytic differentiation    Image Title: Figure 2. Presence of Lymphocytosis With Plasmacytoid Features in Cerebrospinal Fluid    Image Description: Light microscopy of CSF showing lymphocytosis with plasmacytoid features",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Intractable Hiccups with Leptomeningeal Enhancement and Cranial Nerve Involvement",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
